  "meta": {
    "title": "Why Afghanistan Has a Long Thin Arm 🇦🇫🤯 The Wakhan Corridor",
    "description": "Afghanistan 🇦🇫 has a strange long arm reaching east: the Wakhan Corridor, about 350 km long and in places only 13 km wide 📏 It separates Tajikistan 🇹🇯 from Pakistan 🇵🇰 and touches China 🇨🇳 Why? In the 1800s the Russian Empire and British India were racing for Central Asia in the \"Great Game\" ♟️ Agreements in 1873, 1893 and 1895 left this thin strip to Afghanistan as a buffer, so the two empires would never touch. Today about 18,000 people live there, high in the Pamir mountains 🏔️",
    "pinnedComment": [
      "A 13 km wide strip made so two empires would never touch 🤯🇦🇫",
      "Would you travel to the Wakhan? 🏔️👇",
      "Which weird country shape next? 🗺️"
    ],
